WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.387
Pairwise comparison.

2
00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:06.936
Sometimes the question is comparative by nature: is the new prompt better than the old one?

3
00:00:07.216 --> 00:00:09.819
Is model B better than model A on our traffic?

4
00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:14.920
A pairwise judge reads the input and two outputs and picks the better one, or a tie.

5
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:28.107
Eugene Yan’s survey notes that pairwise judgments tend to be more reliable than scoring each output alone for subjective criteria such as tone or persuasiveness, because “which of these is better?

6
00:00:28.387 --> 00:00:32.483
” is an easier question than placing one output on an absolute scale.

7
00:00:32.763 --> 00:00:39.909
Direct scoring remains the better fit for objective, per-output checks such as faithfulness or policy compliance.

8
00:00:40.189 --> 00:00:46.184
Pairwise judging has one bias you must design around from the start: the order of the two answers.

9
00:00:46.464 --> 00:01:01.056
Zheng and colleagues (2023), in the paper that introduced MT-Bench and studied judges systematically, found that GPT-4 gave the same verdict after the two answers were swapped only 65 percent of the time.

10
00:01:01.336 --> 00:01:15.565
Wang and colleagues (2023) showed how much this can matter: with ChatGPT as the judge, simply changing the order made Vicuna-13B beat ChatGPT on 66 of 80 test questions.

11
00:01:15.845 --> 00:01:19.536
The standard defenses, from both papers: Judge both orders.

12
00:01:19.816 --> 00:01:26.152
Zheng and colleagues count a win only if the same answer wins in both orders, and call anything else a tie.

13
00:01:26.432 --> 00:01:30.805
Wang and colleagues average over orders (balanced position calibration).

14
00:01:31.085 --> 00:01:35.565
Allow ties, so the judge isn’t forced to pick between two equally good answers.

15
00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:37.317
Strip origin labels.

16
00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:42.077
Don’t tell the judge which answer is new, baseline, reference, or human-written.

17
00:01:42.357 --> 00:01:45.643
Judging both orders doubles the cost of a pairwise eval.

18
00:01:45.923 --> 00:01:53.347
That is the price of a number you can trust, and the exercise below shows how much a one-order eval can overstate a win.
